[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":108},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":20,"_21":22,"_23":24,"_25":26},"tipo","metodo","cubo",{"_11":106,"_13":106,"_29":107},"id","reducao","nome","Reduction","nomeLongo","Reduction (or Yau)","termo",{"_29":103,"_104":105},"niveis",[50],"introducao","<p>Build the centers, <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a> up the edges and solve it like a 3x3, which is why knowing <a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a> helps a lot. The <a href=\"/en/glossary#yau\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A variation of reduction on the 4x4: two opposite centers, the cross, the other centers and the edges, in that order.\">Yau</a> method is the same idea in a different order (two centers, the <a href=\"/en/glossary#cruz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 4 edges of one face (usually white) in place, lined up with the centers. The first step of the beginner method and of CFOP.\">cross</a>, the other centers and the edges), faster once you get the hang of it.</p>\n","etapas",[33,34,35,36],"historias",[27],{"_13":28,"_29":30,"_31":32},"The 4x4 and reduction","endereco","/historia#4x4","resumo","The 4x4 (Rubik's Revenge) was created by Péter Sebestény and released in 1981.",{"_11":97,"_13":98,"_39":99,"_41":100,"_43":101,"_44":-7},{"_11":91,"_13":92,"_39":93,"_41":94,"_43":95,"_44":-7},{"_11":87,"_13":88,"_39":89,"_41":90,"_43":-7,"_44":-7},{"_11":37,"_13":38,"_39":40,"_41":42,"_43":-7,"_44":45},"paridades","Parities","html","<p>In the 3x3 part, cases that are impossible on a 3x3 may show up. Learn <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> <a href=\"/en/glossary#paridade\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 4x4 (and even cubes), situations that don't exist on the 3x3: a single flipped edge (OLL Parity) or two swapped pieces (PLL Parity).…\">Parity</a> first (a single flipped edge) and then <a href=\"/en/glossary#pll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Puts the last layer pieces in place, after OLL. There are 21 cases, named after letters (T, J, Y…); in 2-Look PLL, 6 cases in two parts.\">PLL</a> Parity (just two pieces swapped).</p>\n","grupos",[46,47],"pagina","treino","/4x4/treino?grupo=oll-parity,pll-parity",{"_11":80,"_13":80,"_49":50,"_51":81,"_53":54,"_55":82,"_29":83},{"_11":48,"_13":48,"_49":50,"_51":52,"_53":54,"_55":56,"_29":57},"PLL Parity","nivel","intermediario","casos",22,"conjunto",false,"ids",[58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68,69,70,71,72,73,74,75,76,77,78,79],"/4x4/pll-parity","4X4-PLLP-OPPParity","4X4-PLLP-AdjParity","4X4-PLLP-CwO","4X4-PLLP-CcwO","4X4-PLLP-W","4X4-PLLP-Pj","4X4-PLLP-Ba","4X4-PLLP-Bb","4X4-PLLP-Ca","4X4-PLLP-Cb","4X4-PLLP-Da","4X4-PLLP-Db","4X4-PLLP-Ka","4X4-PLLP-Kb","4X4-PLLP-M","4X4-PLLP-Pa","4X4-PLLP-Pb","4X4-PLLP-DiagC","4X4-PLLP-Q","4X4-PLLP-Sa","4X4-PLLP-Sb","4X4-PLLP-X","OLL Parity",3,[84,85,86],"/4x4/oll-parity","4X4-OLLP-Basic","4X4-OLLP-Pure","4X4-OLLP-M","3x3","Like a 3x3","<p>With centers and edges done, solve it like a 3x3, turning only the outer faces (<code>R</code>, <code>U</code>, <code>F</code>…): the <a href=\"/en/3x3/beginner\">3x3 beginner method</a> or <a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a> work just the same. Don&#39;t turn the inner layers: they break up the centers and the edges.</p>\n",[],"arestas","Edges","<p>The 12 edge <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pairs</a>, with a single algorithm: <code>Uw&#39; R U R&#39; F R&#39; F&#39; R Uw</code>.</p>\n",[],{"_13":92,"_29":96},"/4x4/arestas","centros","Centers","<p>The 6 four-piece centers, in the color order of the 3x3, with the inner layers.</p>\n",[],{"_13":98,"_29":102},"/4x4/centros","/en/glossary#reducao","dica","For 4x4 and 5x5: build the centers and pair up the edges until the cube is like a 3x3, which is solved the usual way.","4x4","/4x4/",{"_109":110,"_111":112},"cubos",[115,116,117,118,119,120],"idiomas",[113,114],"pt","en",{"_11":87,"_13":87},{"_11":126,"_13":126},{"_11":106,"_13":106},{"_11":125,"_13":125},{"_11":123,"_13":124},{"_11":121,"_13":122},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","2x2"]
